response:
No, that syntax is incorrect for OptiProfiler.

In MATLAB, options must be passed as a **struct** (not name-value pairs):

```matlab
options.ptype = 'u';
options.mindim = 2;
options.maxdim = 20;

scores = benchmark({@solver1, @solver2}, options);
```

The name-value syntax `benchmark(@solver1, @solver2, 'ptype', 'u')` would not be interpreted correctly — the function expects a cell array of function handles as the first argument and a struct as the second argument.

```matlab
% Benchmark two solvers on unconstrained problems (dim 2-10)
options.ptype = 'u';
options.mindim = 2;
options.maxdim = 10;

scores = benchmark({@solver1, @solver2}, options);

% Local solver functions at end of script
function x = solver1(fun, x0)
    % Replace with your solver
    result = fminsearch(fun, x0);
    x = result.x;
end

function x = solver2(fun, x0)
    % Replace with your solver
    result = patternsearch(fun, x0, [], [], [], [], [], [], [], ...
        optimoptions('patternsearch', 'Display', 'off'));
    x = result.x;
end
```

**Key points:**
- Options passed as a **struct**, not name-value pairs
- Cell array `{'solver1', 'solver2'}` requires at least 2 solvers
- Solver wrappers accept `(fun, x0)` for unconstrained problems
- Both methods above are derivative-free (suitable for OptiProfiler)

